  "body": "What happens: a Deburr operation that takes the stock as its source cuts about 0.94 mm into a part, and the setup posts G-code. The part was there at generation. No refusal shows.\n\nExpected: the post is refused, and the refusal names the operation and the part.\n\nEvidence:\n- The safety review of PR #2312 (head `bd69a21770fbeb68efbe8bbcb4d4307367a7077a`) found this with a probe test on aligned stock. The comment is on that PR.\n- The same result occurs on `main` at `3f41b8da6`. PR #2312 does not change it.\n- Possible cause, from the review: the stock simulation gives the edge-break credit (#303) to the Deburr bevel. Thus the simulation finds no gouge. When the reviewer added `Deburr` to the match in `crates/app/src/stock_sim_breaks.rs:36` (a file of PR #2312), the simulation refused the post.\n- A Deburr has no stock-source part law today. A Deburr on a stock made from the model can be a legal edge break. The fix must keep that case legal.\n\nFound during the review of PR #2312 (issue #2308). The two defects have the same family: a stock-source edge operation gets a credit that hides a gouge.\n\nDone means:\n- A test pins that a stock-source Deburr that cuts into a part does not post, and that the refusal names the operation and the part. The test uses aligned stock and stock turned a quarter about Z.\n- A test pins that a legal Deburr of a part edge still posts.\n",
